      "data": {
        "text": "**Initiating Project Structure**\n\nI'm starting to structure the multi-file Python project. I've read `synthesize_text.py` to grasp its functionality, so I'm now ready to generate the remaining eight files. I'm focusing on ensuring these new files are modular and work seamlessly with the existing `synthesize_text.py` for text-to-speech.\n\n\n**Defining Provider Strategy**\n\nI've moved on to defining provider strategies. I'm focusing on identifying concrete external providers for each \"scored\" file, starting with text-to-speech synthesis (e.g., Google Cloud Text-to-Speech, AWS Polly). I need to ensure consistency. I'm also considering cloud storage for audio saving, like AWS S3 or Google Cloud Storage.\n\n\n**Mapping Provider Solutions**\n\nI'm now mapping out concrete providers. For `synthesize_ssml.py`, I'm considering Google Cloud Text-to-Speech, AWS Polly, or Azure Cognitive Services Speech. I'm also planning the `select_voice.py` and aiming for consistent providers. `save_audio.py` will use standard file I/O for local file saving, or I may select a cloud storage solution for object storage. I've also identified potential providers for translation, content safety, and embeddings, as well as a speech-to-text service for the final file. I'm focusing on ensuring modularity.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing File Structure**\n\nI've carefully read `synthesize_text.py` and am ready to generate the remaining files. I'm focusing now on provider selection for consistency. For instance, I'll aim to use the same provider for both `synthesize_ssml.py` and `select_voice.py` to streamline integration. The helper files, `speech_client.py` and `speech_models.py`, will be next.\n\n\n",
